.Dd September 13, 2026
.Dt MORIARTY\-TEST\-RULES 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm moriarty\-test\-rules
.Nd evaluate a rule regression suite against an explicit policy
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm moriarty
.Cm test rules
.Ar suite
.Fl c Ar config
.Op Fl \-cwd Ar directory
.Op Fl \-json
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Cm test rules
command parses a caller-owned TOML suite, completely prepares it,
compiles the bash and tool engines once, and evaluates every expanded row
sequentially in the current process.
Commands and tools represented by fixture data are never executed.
.Pp
.Ar suite
and
.Ar config
are required paths.
Relative paths are resolved from the process invocation directory.
The command never falls back to the deployed XDG policy.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl c , Fl \-config Ar config
Select the candidate
.Xr moriarty\-tool\-rules 5
and
.Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5
policy.
This option is required.
.It Fl \-cwd Ar directory
Set the default evaluation directory.
An absolute path is used directly;
a relative path is joined to the invocation directory.
The default is the invocation directory.
This option does not change the process directory or suite/config path lookup.
.It Fl \-json
Write one complete JSON report instead of the human report.
.El
.Sh SUITE FORMAT
The top level is strict TOML with numeric format version 1 and a nonempty
case array:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
format_version = 1

[[cases]]
id = "filesystem/remove-root"
name = "Removing root must not auto-allow"
request = { kind = "bash", command = "rm -rf /" }
expect = "not_auto_allowed"

[[cases]]
id = "tool/null-async"
name = "Null is distinct from absence"
request = { kind = "tool", tool = "subagent", input_json = '{"async":null}' }
modes = ["default"]
expect = "denied"
reason = "async must be true"
.Ed
.Pp
These are caller-owned assertions, not a policy or suite bundled with Moriarty.
Unknown top-level, case, and request fields are errors.
.Bl -tag -width expand_cwd
.It Ic format_version
Required numeric value 1.
Other numbers and nonnumeric values are rejected.
.It Ic cases
Required nonempty array of case tables.
.It Ic id
Required nonempty identifier, unique within the suite.
.It Ic name
Required nonempty display name.
Names need not be unique.
.It Ic request
Required tagged inline table.
.Ic kind = "bash"
requires a nonempty
.Ic command .
.Ic kind = "tool"
requires a nonempty
.Ic tool
other than
.Ql Bash
and an
.Ic input_json
string containing any valid JSON value.
JSON nulls and literal dotted keys are preserved.
.It Ic expect
Required closed assertion:
.Cm allowed , ask , denied , no_match ,
or
.Cm not_auto_allowed .
.It Ic modes
Optional nonempty list using exactly
.Cm default , plan , acceptEdits , auto , dontAsk ,
or
.Cm bypassPermissions .
Duplicates are rejected.
Rows expand in case and listed-mode order.
Omission creates one mode-less row,
in which only unrestricted rules participate.
.It Ic cwd
Optional case evaluation directory.
A relative value is joined to the default evaluation directory.
.It Ic reason
Optional only with
.Cm denied .
It must equal the actual denial reason byte for byte.
Omission accepts any denial reason;
an empty value requires an empty actual reason.
.It Ic expand_cwd
Optional boolean, default false.
When true, the request must contain an exact
.Ql {{cwd}}
marker.
The marker is replaced non-recursively in a Bash command or JSON string
values, never JSON object keys.
Without opt-in it remains literal.
.El
.Sh ASSERTIONS
An allowed command, a successfully revalidated argument-filter rewrite,
or an allowed tool satisfies
.Cm allowed .
Asked, denied, and unmatched results satisfy their same-named assertions.
Asked, denied, and unmatched results also satisfy
.Cm not_auto_allowed .
A non-Bash tool no-match means hook pass-through, not Ask.
.Pp
A Bash
.Cm modified
result satisfies no version-1 assertion; its rewrite remains in the report.
When
.Ic reason
is present, the result must also be an actual denial with the exact reason.
.Sh PREPARATION AND EVALUATION
Moriarty validates the default cwd, reads and strictly parses the entire
suite, and prepares every case before loading the candidate policy.
A late invalid case therefore prevents all evaluation and report rendering.
Every effective cwd must be an existing directory with a valid UTF-8 path.
Fixture paths are not created.
.Pp
The candidate policy is loaded once.
Both engines use their diagnostic compilers;
any dropped-rule diagnostic rejects the policy rather than silently
weakening it.
Each Bash row receives fresh evaluation and alias state while sharing the
compiled engine.
.Pp
Bash rows use completed offline Bash decision evaluation,
not generic PreToolUse dispatch or a tool-rule short circuit.
Tool rows use checked evaluation,
so locality infrastructure errors abort the run.
The live hook intentionally falls back when that infrastructure fails.
.Sh OUTPUT
JSON output directly serializes one report object containing:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
numeric
.Ic format_version ;
.It
.Ic source_case_count , evaluation_count , passed_count ,
and
.Ic failed_count ;
.It
every ordered row, including passes.
.El
.Pp
Each row contains
.Ic id , name , mode , effective_cwd , request , expected , actual ,
and
.Ic passed .
.Ic mode
is null for a mode-less row.
.Ic expected_reason
is omitted only when absent; an explicit empty string remains present.
Tool requests contain parsed
.Ic input
rather than the original JSON string.
Actual results retain applicable rule, reason, and rewrite data.
.Pp
Human output prints only failed rows, followed by one line with all four counts.
IDs, names, cwds, requests, expected reasons, and actual details are escaped
so embedded controls do not create ambiguous row boundaries.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width 2
.It 0
A nonempty suite completed and every expanded row passed.
.It 1
Evaluation completed with one or more assertion mismatches.
The complete report is written before exit,
including failures after an earlier mismatch.
.It 2
CLI, file, parse, preparation, policy compilation,
evaluation infrastructure, or report output failed.
A failure before rendering writes diagnostics to standard error and no report
to standard output.
.El
.Sh LIMITATIONS
Results may depend on the host filesystem and
.Ev HOME
because existing rule path handling does.
The runner does not create fixture paths and makes no accuracy guarantee
under concurrent filesystem mutation.
Offline Bash evaluation and checked tool evaluation can establish bounded
semantic parity with the live hook,
but the command is not a universal hook emulator.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Evaluate a suite and print only failed rows plus totals:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
moriarty test rules suite.toml --config candidate.toml
.Ed
.Pp
Evaluate relative to a project directory and retain all rows as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
moriarty test rules suite.toml -c candidate.toml --cwd project --json
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr moriarty 1 ,
.Xr moriarty\-bash\-rules 5 ,
.Xr moriarty\-tool\-rules 5
